        'Dual-task interference exploits the limited cognitive-motor reserve of a recovering post-concussion brain. Single-task tandem gait may appear normal in a client who is near-recovered — the brain can dedicate sufficient resources to gait alone. Adding a simultaneous cognitive demand (counting backwards) exceeds available processing capacity, revealing the residual deficit as slower time, more step-off errors, or both. Serial improvement in dual-task interference — the shrinking gap between single- and dual-task performance — is an objective, trackable signal of advancing recovery and a concrete finding to include in progress communications to the referring practitioner.',
